**Model (Working storage and chain substitutes)** · `model:design:functional-mocks` The storage model executes SCD2 reads and transactions over immutable rows with explicit scheduling, rollback and conflict decisions; the burn model executes the development asset ledger, target-committed destruction, settlement depth and provenance with controllable forks. Both are functional implementations of the same public contracts tested against real services, including their refusals, not convenient answers substituted beneath assertions (`model:harness:mocks`). Generic test-only models live in accepted pn-testkit; thin adapters in the owning crate's tests and pn-verification instantiate public carriers without importing production code into the model. Valid simulated provenance uses declared public fixtures or ephemeral test keys held in secret types; fabricated proof success is not allowed. The shared suite checks exact amounts, target attribution, histories and outcomes; real-only transport, crash and signature cases retain separate required claims. No test model supplies binding deployment inputs or proves economic assurance.

**Signature (Public client harness)** · `sig:design:client` pn-client accepts CBOR-native GraphQL and returns GraphQL, using the node pn's schema as an unchanged subset plus client workflow roots (`dec:harness:client-schema`). Its one byte execution entry point is an embedding boundary, not a parallel Rust API for faucet, burn, submit or read. Relayed node operations preserve node outcomes, errors and exact canonical document bytes; pn-interchange supplies strict document decoding and bounded message reading on both sides. Client workflows cover faucet funding, burn-new-account, burn-existing-account and recovery; each has explicit stages, independent local limits and uncertain-effect outcomes under (`rule:api:limits`), (`rule:api:outcomes`). pn-wallet owns wallet funding and all spend construction, signing, broadcast and recovery via Elements RPC, with node funding calls transported as GraphQL by pn-client through an injected channel. The client asks pn-wallet for the transaction already associated with the request before any new spend; no client PostgreSQL or local journal exists (`rule:harness:wallet-recovery`). Validate the run and authenticated permanent target before irreversible work. Existing spendable tokens may fund another account's fresh burn, but old burn amount never moves. Private custody and full public observation follow (`rec:observe:decision`). Package roles and directions are decided by (`dec:harness:client-packages`); added placement is accepted under (`proposal:harness:packages`), while implementation evidence remains held.

**Signature (Development wallet)** · `sig:design:wallet` pn-wallet owns the test-asset workflow and its state in the Elements wallet. Bind each run/request to immutable intent, chosen inputs and exact transaction, validate the signed permanent-target and request commitment, serialize all wallet writers and recover through the wallet under (`rule:harness:wallet-recovery`). Funding chooses a receiving destination and preserves the node grant request; a lost grant reply is resolved against node status and wallet receipt, not replaced by another grant. Wallet comments and transaction summaries are not independent burn evidence. An incomplete scan, locked/unavailable wallet, crash between creation and association or ambiguous conflict produces unavailable or incomplete with unknown effect; do not spend again or claim rollback. Persist prepared account binding with the wallet request or recover it from its existing public receipt, so a lost reply cannot silently create another account. The admitted RPC integration must prove durable association and complete lookup before spending is enabled; otherwise report unsupported. This is an implementation hold, not an assertion that stock RPC methods already provide atomic request handling. Node quota/history storage remains separate under (`rule:devnet:history`). This is the sole Elements RPC boundary for pn-host and pn-client, covering chain/profile inspection, raw burn evidence, settlement checks and faucet spending as well as client burns. Host retains quota/history transactions; pn-wallet retains transaction authority and recovery. Its chain slice lands in bite 2 and its spending/client workflows in bite 8 under (`amend:decomposition:infrastructure`). Admit the integration once with secret-preserving state/errors, while each caller retains its own authorization and independent conformance obligations. The first slice uses cookie-authenticated bounded HTTP through the secret-owning transport and the evaluated exact decoder (`amend:devnet:wallet-cookie`), (`amend:devnet:rpc-bounds`), (`amend:devnet:wallet-decoder`). It reads chain evidence only; later signing is delegated to the Elements wallet, with local cryptographic operation admission governed by (`amend:crypto:wallet-operation-scope`) and (`amend:crypto:wallet-codec-features`). Its read-only cookie is not a restricted server identity; decide that identity before adding spending methods (`open:devnet:spending-rpc-identity`).

**Signature (Node and client command)** · `sig:design:command` pn-command has node operations start, inspect and administer, plus client operations faucet, burn-new, burn-existing, submit, read, fetch and recover. It depends on pn's library surface and pn-client, invoking only their CBOR-native GraphQL execution boundaries; cli-common owns JSON presentation and tracing (`rule:harness:client-output`). Local node start uses the control executor before an HTTP service exists; remote public endpoints gain no administrative operations or secret-management fields. Validate authorization and private bootstrap context through secret carriers before effects, preserve node/client origin and execution stage in the result, and never translate operational uncertainty into a mathematical verdict. A successful command emits one JSON result and newline, stderr carries JSON traces, and exact document artifacts retain their bytes. No direct Elements RPC, PostgreSQL recovery store or separate business-operation protocol is introduced in the command.

**Verification (Client workflows)** · `verif:design:client-workflows` Planned and held under (`open:design:harness-evidence`) and (`open:design:claim-evidence`). Execute the same GraphQL client-contract cases through functional mocks and the actual pn API with real PostgreSQL/Elements; the PostgreSQL instance belongs to the node, not the client. Check schema-subset equality, client workflow roots, mixed-operation refusal, exact relayed document bytes and unchanged node outcomes against independent producers. Test both command surfaces through their GraphQL execution entry points, including local start before HTTP availability and refusal of unauthorized administration. Cover faucet refusal outside the profile, new-account preparation, fresh burns to the same existing account, gift burns to another account without moving prior value, missing/changed target commitments, wrong runs, settlement, exact package bytes and independently checked replay inputs. Inject loss before and after wallet creation, request association, signing and broadcast; restart the wallet and client, race callers with the same and conflicting intents, and assert that recovery never issues a second payment. Include wrong-wallet selection, incomplete/paginated history, lagging mempool views, abandoned/conflicted transactions, durable association loss, rejected account preparation and stale bindings. An incomplete wallet answer must report uncertainty; unsupported atomic association refuses spending before effects. Cross-check actual transaction commitments and use narrower pn-wallet test-site claims to support the integrated client claim, without treating the RPC summary as its own oracle. Subprocess cases check record 004 streams, terminal refusal before mutation, exit classes and full public observation with secret-safe error paths. Seeded histories shrink while preserving the target and failure premise; independent raw transaction checks establish that the selected target and destroyed amount agree. The prospective test-site claim is in (`ledger:design:claim-plan`), not minted here.

